window.docContents[126].resolve({"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Weak-derivatives___-completeness___-and-approximation":{"contents":"Weak derivatives are defined by the distributional identity\n\n\n\nfor every compactly supported smooth test function.  On open domains the weak\nderivative is unique almost everywhere.  The graph-space compatibility\nconditions are closed under limits, which makes the bundled graph space\ncomplete and supports bounded-operator arguments.\n\nThe weak-derivative relation also accepts values in any real Banach space.\nThe test function remains real-valued, and the integrals are Bochner integrals.\nOn every open Euclidean domain, the derivative is unique almost everywhere\nand its graph in  is closed for every\n. The scalar relation is recovered exactly when the\ncodomain is .\n\nThis closed graph also gives the complete space  for\nevery natural order , every open Euclidean domain, and every real Banach\nspace , including infinite-dimensional codomains. At ,\nthe scalar identification preserves every derivative coordinate and the\ngraph norm exactly. A continuous linear map  acts on these spaces\nby composition and commutes with their weak derivatives.\n\nFor , smooth functions are dense in this graph norm.\nOn the whole space, we convolve with a scalar mollifier and prove that each\nweak derivative passes through the convolution. On an arbitrary open set,\nwe first localize with a smooth partition and mollify each piece inside the\ndomain. The locally finite sum gives a smooth approximation without imposing\nregularity on the boundary. The argument works for any real Banach codomain;\nit does not require finite dimension.\n\nFor scalar-valued functions, the approximation layer has several distinct scopes.\n\n* On the whole space and at finite exponent, convolution with standard\n  mollifiers gives smooth approximation in the Sobolev graph norm.* On a domain, zero extension followed by convolution gives an interior\n  approximation.  It is not automatically a global approximation up to the\n  boundary.* On any open domain, a locally finite partition and interior mollification\n  prove smooth density in the full  norm for .\n  The approximating functions are smooth on the domain; they need not extend\n  smoothly across its boundary.* A representative-level extension hypothesis transfers whole-space\n  approximation back to a domain.* An operational first-order extension operator gives density for the\n  corresponding completed domain graph space.* The zero-boundary spaces are defined as closures of the compactly supported\n  smooth core, so density is part of their construction.\n\nNorm density at  is false in general, and density up to the\nboundary requires the appropriate extension or zero-boundary hypothesis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.2. Weak derivatives, completeness, and approximation","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Weak-derivatives___-completeness___-and-approximation"}});